1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:06,762
Lahori Gate is architecturally described as a magnificent three-storeyed gateway structure.

2
00:00:06,712 --> 00:00:12,550
The gate is clad in red sandstone across its massive outer elevation.

3
00:00:12,550 --> 00:00:17,162
It is situated on the western wall of the Red Fort monument.

4
00:00:17,162 --> 00:00:22,800
The gateway is flanked by tall semi-octagonal towers on either side.

5
00:00:22,800 --> 00:00:28,550
These semi-octagonal towers are crowned by two open octagonal pavilions.

6
00:00:28,550 --> 00:00:36,212
While the main structure uses red sandstone, the roofs of these octagonal pavilions use white stone.

7
00:00:36,212 --> 00:00:40,750
The facade of the gateway is decorated with square panels.

8
00:00:40,750 --> 00:00:45,200
It is also decorated with rectangular decorative panels.

9
00:00:45,200 --> 00:00:50,362
Series of cusped arched panels further ornament the stone surface.

10
00:00:50,362 --> 00:00:56,250
Emperor Aurangzeb later added an outer barbican screening the main gateway.

11
00:00:56,250 --> 00:01:01,912
From the gate, the approach path leads into Chhatta-Chowk, a roofed passage.

12
00:01:01,912 --> 00:01:07,237
This covered passage is flanked by double-storeyed arcaded apartments.

13
00:01:07,237 --> 00:01:15,375
The architectural contrast between red sandstone walls and white stone pavilion roofs defines its skyline.

14
00:01:15,375 --> 00:01:22,112
Semi-octagonal towers provide symmetry and verticality to the three-storeyed entrance facade.

15
00:01:22,112 --> 00:01:29,200
Square, rectangular, and cusped arched panels articulate the stone surface of the gateway.

16
00:01:29,200 --> 00:01:35,875
The roofed arcade of Chhatta-Chowk extends the architecture into a covered double-storeyed walkway.

17
00:01:35,875 --> 00:01:42,950
Together, these recorded elements constitute the complete architectural design of Lahori Gate.

